<DocsLayout title="Install" pathname="/docs/install" section="Getting started">
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p class="dx-lede">
    One command turns any git repo into a Máddu workspace &mdash; runtime, cockpit, and
    state directory scaffolded in under a minute, touching nothing else in your tree.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <p>Two things, both likely already in place:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>git repository</strong> &mdash; run the install from its root.</li>
    <li><strong>Node.js &ge; 20</strong> on your <code>PATH</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install command</h2>
  <p>
    Máddu installs straight from the public GitHub repo. No auth, no account &mdash; the
    repo is public, so the command runs for anyone. The npm package is on its way; until it
    ships, the GitHub path is canonical, and both produce identical doctor output.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-code">
    <div class="dx-code-head"><span class="dx-code-dot"></span> terminal</div>
    <pre><code><span class="tok-c"># install the latest from GitHub</span>
npx github:frdyx/maddu init

<span class="tok-c"># verify the install + hard rules</span>
maddu doctor</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>What it creates</h2>
  <p>The installer scaffolds exactly three paths and leaves the rest of your tree untouched:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>.maddu/</code> &mdash; the state directory: config, the append-only spine, and projections.</li>
    <li><code>maddu/</code> &mdash; the runtime and cockpit you drive the framework with.</li>
    <li><code>maddu.json</code> &mdash; the workspace manifest.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">&#9670;</span>
    <p><strong>Nothing else moves.</strong> No files outside those three paths are added or changed, so the install is safe to run in an existing project.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Verify</h2>
  <p>
    Run <code>maddu doctor</code> to check install integrity and the hard rules against
    Máddu's own files. A fresh install on a foreign repo returns <strong>77 pass &middot; 1
    warn &middot; 0 fail</strong> &mdash; the single warn is just &ldquo;no project test run
    yet.&rdquo; If doctor fails, surface it and stop rather than papering over it.
  </p>

  <h2>Upgrading</h2>
  <p>
    If <code>.maddu/</code> already exists, this is an upgrade, not a fresh install &mdash;
    <code>init</code> refuses to overwrite an existing state directory. Run the upgrade path
    instead:
  </p>

  <div class="dx-code">
    <div class="dx-code-head"><span class="dx-code-dot"></span> terminal</div>
    <pre><code><span class="tok-c"># already have .maddu/ ? upgrade in place</span>
maddu upgrade</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Optional: session hooks</h2>
  <p>
    On Claude Code, you can wire session discipline so a repo never starts building
    unrecorded. This installs <code>SessionStart</code> and <code>SessionEnd</code> hooks
    &mdash; auto-registering a session and recording it to the spine, then closing it &mdash;
    idempotently, without disturbing existing hooks.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-code">
    <div class="dx-code-head"><span class="dx-code-dot"></span> terminal</div>
    <pre><code><span class="tok-c"># wire SessionStart / SessionEnd into Claude Code</span>
maddu hooks install</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    After that a single auto-registered session flows into <code>lane claim</code> and
    <code>slice-stop</code> with no extra flags. The hooks are Claude Code&ndash;specific;
    skip this step on other runtimes.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-pager">
